From: as7760 bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] mpc85xx: chainload: restart secondaries from our boot page

With CHAINLOAD_CCSR_RELOCATED the secondary cores are already spinning in the
previous U-Boot's boot page near the top of RAM, which relocation overwrites
(seen on an AS7760: "CPU up timeout", then Linux oopsing on corrupted memory).
Pulse their MPIC reset once boot page translation points at our own spin loop.

--- a/arch/powerpc/cpu/mpc85xx/mp.c	2026-11-14 13:55:55.593201223 -0800
+++ b/arch/powerpc/cpu/mpc85xx/mp.c	2026-11-14 14:01:40.280080580 -0800
@@ -282,6 +282,28 @@
 	/* readback to sync write */
 	in_be32(&ccm->bstrar);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_CHAINLOAD_CCSR_RELOCATED
+	/*
+	 * The previous loader already released the secondaries into its own
+	 * spin loop, which relocation has overwritten. Pulse their reset (one
+	 * MPIC PIR bit per hardware thread, never our own core's threads):
+	 * boot release is still set, so they restart from our boot page.
+	 */
+	{
+		int tpc = CONFIG_SYS_FSL_THREADS_PER_CORE;
+		u32 rst = ((1 << (cpu_numcores() * tpc)) - 1) & ~((1 << tpc) - 1);
+		int n;
+
+		printf("chainload: restarting secondaries (PIR 0x%x)\n", rst);
+		out_be32(&pic->pir, rst);
+		(void)in_be32(&pic->pir);
+		udelay(10);
+		out_be32(&pic->pir, 0);
+		for (n = 0; n < 15; n++)
+			(void)in_be32(&pic->pir);
+	}
+#endif
+
 	/* disable time base at the platform */
 	out_be32(&rcpm->ctbenrl, cpu_up_mask);
